.TH OKAGENT 1 "2026-09-18" "okagent 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
okagent \- SSH agent backed by an OnlyKey hardware token
.SH SYNOPSIS
.SY okagent
.RI [ options ]
.B status
.YS
.SY okagent
.RI [ options ]
.B pubkey
.RB [ \-\-pubkey\-file
.IR file ]
.RI [ identity .\|.\|.]
.YS
.SY okagent
.RI [ options ]
.B run
.RB [ \-\-pubkey\-file
.IR file ]
.RI [ identity .\|.\|.]
.B \-\-
.I command
.RI [ arg .\|.\|.]
.YS
.SY okagent
.RI [ options ]
.B shell
.RB [ \-\-pubkey\-file
.IR file ]
.RI [ identity .\|.\|.]
.YS
.SY okagent
.RI [ options ]
.B ssh
.I identity
.RI [ ssh-arg .\|.\|.]
.YS
.SY okagent
.RI [ options ]
.B serve
.RB [ \-\-socket
.IR path ]
.RB [ \-\-daemon ]
.RB [ \-\-pubkey\-file
.IR file ]
.RI [ identity .\|.\|.]
.YS
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B okagent
is an SSH agent whose private keys live on an
.UR https://onlykey.io
OnlyKey
.UE
hardware token.
No key material is stored on the host: for every operation the token
re-derives the key from an
.I identity
string such as
.BR james@example.com ,
and every signature must be confirmed on the device by entering a
three-digit challenge on its buttons.
.PP
Given the same identity and curve,
.B okagent
derives the same public key as the Python
.B onlykey\-agent
program, so existing
.I authorized_keys
entries keep working.
.PP
Keys that the OnlyKey app has written into one of the token's sixteen ECC
slots or four RSA slots ("stored keys") can be used as well, by naming the
slot with
.B \-\-slot
or the
.B slot
configuration key.
The identity then only labels the key; it is not involved in signing.
RSA keys exist only as stored keys.
.PP
An identity has the form
.RI [ user @] host .
A
.IB scheme ://
prefix, a
.BI : port
suffix and a
.BI / path
suffix are accepted and ignored.
Non-ASCII characters are transliterated to ASCII before the identity is
hashed; a warning is logged when this happens.
Identities may be omitted from the command line when the configuration file
lists them (see
.BR "CONFIGURATION FILE" ).
.PP
The agent implements only the
.I request identities
and
.I sign request
operations of the SSH agent protocol.
Adding, removing or locking keys is refused, which OpenSSH reports as
unsupported.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B status
Print the firmware version and lock state of the attached OnlyKey.
.TP
.BI pubkey " " [ identity \fR.\|.\|.]
Print the public key of each identity, one per line, in
.I authorized_keys
format.
The comment field is
.BI <ssh:// identity | curve >
where
.I curve
is
.B ed25519
or
.BR nist256p1 ,
matching the Python agent.
A stored key's comment has the slot as a third field,
.BI <ssh:// identity | curve | ECC n >
or
.BI <ssh:// identity |rsa|RSA n >,
so that a derived and a stored key for the same identity can be told apart.
.TP
.BI run " " [ identity \fR.\|.\|.] " \-\- " command " " [ arg \fR.\|.\|.]
Start a temporary agent on a private socket, run
.I command
with
.B SSH_AUTH_SOCK
pointing at it, and stop the agent when the command exits.
.B okagent
exits with the command's exit status.
.TP
.BI shell " " [ identity \fR.\|.\|.]
Like
.BR run ,
but runs
.B $SHELL
(or
.I /bin/sh
if unset).
.TP
.BI ssh " identity " [ ssh-arg \fR.\|.\|.]
Derive the identity's key and run
.BR ssh (1)
against it.
The host part of the identity is the destination and the user part, if
any, becomes the login name.
.B ssh
is told to use only this key
.RB ( "IdentitiesOnly=yes" )
by way of a temporary public key file.
Any further arguments are passed to
.B ssh
after the destination, so they normally form a remote command.
.TP
.BI serve " " [ identity \fR.\|.\|.]
Run a long-lived agent on a unix socket.
Without
.BR \-\-daemon ,
the agent stays in the foreground and prints a line of the form
.PP
.RS
.RS
.EX
SSH_AUTH_SOCK=\fIpath\fR; export SSH_AUTH_SOCK;
.EE
.RE
.PP
on standard error.
The agent stops on SIGINT, SIGTERM or SIGHUP and removes its socket.
An existing live socket at the path is never replaced.
.RE
.SH OPTIONS
The following options apply to every command and may be given before or
after the command name.
.TP
.BI \-\-config " file"
Read
.I file
instead of the default configuration file.
A missing explicit file is an error; a missing default file is not.
.TP
.BI \-\-curve " curve"
Curve for identities given on the command line, and the default for
configuration entries without one.
.I curve
is
.B ed25519
(the default) or
.B nistp256
(also spelled
.B p256
or
.BR nist256p1 ).
.TP
.BI \-\-slot " slot"
Use the key stored in
.I slot
for the identities given on the command line, instead of deriving one.
.I slot
is
.B ECC1
to
.B ECC16
(a bare number means an ECC slot) or
.B RSA1
to
.BR RSA4 ,
as numbered in the OnlyKey app.
For an ECC slot the
.B \-\-curve
(or its default) must match the key type the slot was written with;
otherwise the command fails and names the curve the slot actually holds.
An RSA slot takes no curve, and giving
.B \-\-curve
with one is an error; whether the slot holds a 2048- or 4096-bit key is
read from the token.
An empty slot is reported as such by the firmware.
This option applies only to identities on the command line; configuration
entries carry their own
.B slot
key.
.TP
.BI \-\-notify\-command " command"
Run
.I command
whenever a signature needs confirming, with the challenge prompt appended
as its last argument.
.I command
is split on whitespace; the first word is the program.
The prompt is still written to the terminal as well.
See
.BR "CHALLENGE PROMPT" .
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Log more on standard error.
Once for informational messages, twice for debug output, three times for
trace output.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print usage and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.PP
The
.BR pubkey ", " run ", " shell " and " serve
commands also accept:
.TP
.BI \-\-pubkey\-file " file"
A file of lines as printed by
.BR "okagent pubkey" .
Keys whose comment matches one of the agent's identities are listed to SSH
clients even while the OnlyKey is unplugged or locked, so that
.B ssh
can choose the right key before the device is unlocked.
Blank lines and lines starting with
.B #
are ignored.
.PP
The
.B serve
command also accepts:
.TP
.BI \-\-socket " path"
Listen on
.I path
instead of the default socket.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-daemon
Fork into the background.
The parent prints the
.B SSH_AUTH_SOCK
shell snippet on standard output, suitable for
.BR eval ,
and exits.
The child detaches from the terminal and redirects standard input and
output to
.IR /dev/null ;
log output still goes to standard error.
.SH CHALLENGE PROMPT
When an SSH client asks for a signature,
.B okagent
writes a line such as
.PP
.RS
.EX
OnlyKey: enter 3 1 5 to sign as james@example.com (ssh-connection login as "james"), or press any button if challenge mode is off
.EE
.RE
.PP
to the controlling terminal
.RI ( /dev/tty ),
or to standard error when there is none.
For a stored key the prompt says
.BI "with stored key ECC" n
or
.BI "with stored key RSA" n
after the identity.
The signature completes once the digits are entered on the token.
If the device does not answer within about 22 seconds, is unplugged, is
locked, or the wrong digits are entered, the request fails and the SSH
client is told so; the agent keeps running.
.PP
A background agent has no useful terminal, so use
.B \-\-notify\-command
or the
.B notify\-command
configuration key to hand the prompt to a program, for example
.BR "notify\-send OnlyKey" .
The program receives the whole prompt as its last argument and the parts
separately in the environment variables
.B OKAGENT_DIGITS
(the three digits separated by spaces),
.B OKAGENT_IDENTITY
and, for a stored key,
.B OKAGENT_SLOT
(for example
.BR ECC3 ).
.PP
Every signature is verified against the public key before it is returned to
the client.
.PP
An RSA key signs a SHA-256 or SHA-512 digest, as chosen by the client's
.B rsa\-sha2\-256
or
.B rsa\-sha2\-512
request; a client asking for the legacy SHA-1
.B ssh\-rsa
signature is refused.
.SH CONFIGURATION FILE
.B okagent
reads
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/okagent/config.toml
(falling back to
.IR ~/.config/okagent/config.toml )
unless
.B \-\-config
or
.B OKAGENT_CONFIG
names another file.
The file is TOML.
Unknown keys are an error.
Command-line options override the corresponding keys.
.TP
.BI curve " = \(dq" curve \(dq
Default curve; see
.BR \-\-curve .
.TP
.BI notify-command " = \(dq" command \(dq
See
.BR \-\-notify\-command .
.TP
.BI socket " = \(dq" path \(dq
Socket path for
.BR serve ;
see
.BR \-\-socket .
.TP
.BI pubkey-file " = \(dq" file \(dq
See
.BR \-\-pubkey\-file .
.TP
.B [[identity]]
One table per identity, used when no identities are given on the command
line.
Each has a required
.B name
key holding the
.RI [ user @] host
string, an optional
.B curve
key that overrides the default curve for that identity only, and an optional
.B slot
key
.RB ( \(dqECC3\(dq ,
the number
.B 3
or
.BR \(dqRSA1\(dq )
that uses the stored key in that slot instead of a derived one; see
.BR \-\-slot .
An RSA slot entry must not have a
.B curve
key.
.PP
Example:
.PP
.RS
.EX
curve = "ed25519"
notify-command = "notify-send OnlyKey"
# socket = "/run/user/1000/okagent/agent.sock"
# pubkey-file = "/home/james/.ssh/onlykey.pub"

[[identity]]
name = "james@example.com"

[[identity]]
name = "git@github.com"
curve = "nistp256"

[[identity]]
name = "james@legacy.example.com"
slot = "ECC3"

[[identity]]
name = "james@old.example.com"
slot = "RSA1"
.EE
.RE
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B OKAGENT_CONFIG
Configuration file to read; same as
.BR \-\-config .
.TP
.B OKAGENT_LOG
Log filter in
.B tracing_subscriber
.B EnvFilter
syntax, for example
.B debug
or
.BR onlykey_agent=trace .
When set it replaces the level chosen by
.BR \-\-verbose .
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Base directory for the default configuration file.
.TP
.B XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
Base directory for sockets.
.TP
.B SHELL
Shell started by
.BR "okagent shell" .
.TP
.B SSH_AUTH_SOCK
Set for the child of
.BR run ", " shell " and " ssh .
Printed by
.B serve
for the user to export.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/okagent/config.toml
Default configuration file.
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/okagent/agent.sock
Default socket for
.BR serve .
If
.B XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
is unset,
.IR /tmp/okagent\- uid /agent.sock
is used instead.
The socket and its directory are created with owner-only permissions.
.TP
.IR $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/okagent/run\- pid .sock
Temporary socket used by
.BR run ", " shell " and " ssh ;
removed on exit.
.TP
.I /dev/hidraw*
The token is opened directly through the Linux hidraw interface.
A udev rule is needed to open it without root; see the OnlyKey
documentation for
.IR 49\-onlykey.rules .
"Permission denied" from
.B okagent status
means the rule is missing.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B okagent
exits 0 on success and 1 on error.
.B run
and
.B shell
exit with the status of the command they ran, or 128 if it was killed by a
signal;
.B ssh
exits with the status of
.BR ssh (1).
.SH EXAMPLES
Publish a key and log in with it:
.PP
.RS
.EX
okagent pubkey james@example.com >> ~/.ssh/authorized_keys.example.com
okagent ssh james@example.com
okagent run james@example.com \-\- ssh example.com
.EE
.RE
.PP
Use a P-256 key for GitHub and run a command with it:
.PP
.RS
.EX
okagent \-\-curve nistp256 pubkey git@github.com
okagent \-\-curve nistp256 run git@github.com \-\- git push
.EE
.RE
.PP
Use an ed25519 key that the OnlyKey app stored in slot 3:
.PP
.RS
.EX
okagent \-\-slot ECC3 pubkey james@legacy.example.com
okagent \-\-slot ECC3 ssh james@legacy.example.com
.EE
.RE
.PP
Use the RSA key the OnlyKey app stored in slot 1:
.PP
.RS
.EX
okagent \-\-slot RSA1 pubkey james@old.example.com
okagent \-\-slot RSA1 ssh james@old.example.com
.EE
.RE
.PP
Start a background agent for the current shell session:
.PP
.RS
.EX
eval "$(okagent serve \-\-daemon james@example.com)"
ssh\-add \-L
.EE
.RE
.PP
Run the agent as a systemd user service with desktop notifications:
.PP
.RS
.EX
# ~/.config/systemd/user/okagent.service
[Unit]
Description=OnlyKey SSH agent

[Service]
ExecStart=%h/.cargo/bin/okagent serve \-\-notify\-command "notify\-send OnlyKey"
Restart=on\-failure

[Install]
WantedBy=default.target
.EE
.RE
.PP
Then
.B systemctl \-\-user enable \-\-now okagent
and
.BR "export SSH_AUTH_SOCK=$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/okagent/agent.sock" .
.SH NOTES
Only the original OnlyKey is supported, not the OnlyKey DUO.
The agent covers SSH only; GPG and age keys are out of scope.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ssh (1),
.BR ssh\-add (1),
.BR ssh\-agent (1),
.BR notify\-send (1)
.PP
